  "policy_title": "Scrap Police and Crime Commissioners and Introduce Police Boards",
  "outcome": "O9",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O9",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy removes a directly elected policing overseer and replaces them with a board of councillors, trading one form of democratic accountability for another. Whether that improves or weakens public democratic rights in policing depends on whether broader local council representation outweighs the loss of a dedicated elected mandate.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether Police Boards embedded in existing local government will provide genuinely stronger democratic accountability than a directly elected PCC, or instead dilute it into a lower-visibility, lower-mandate structure.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy replaces directly elected PCCs with Police Boards made up of councillors and representatives from relevant local groups.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "replacing them with local Police Boards made up of councillors and representatives from relevant local groups"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "PCCs are directly elected officials established to oversee policing, giving voters a dedicated ballot on policing accountability.",
        "evidence_id": "E2",
        "quote": "PCCs are directly elected officials established in 2012 under the Police Reform and Social Responsibility Act 2011 to oversee policing in their areas"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Turnout for PCC elections in 2024 was only 23%, and two in five people were unaware of PCCs' existence, suggesting limited democratic engagement with the current model.",
        "evidence_id": "E22",
        "quote": "turnout for PCC elections in 2024 was only 23%, and two in five people were unaware of PCCs' existence"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Public confidence in local police has fallen from 76% a decade ago to 67% in the year ending March 2025, indicating the existing accountability model has not sustained trust.",
        "evidence_id": "E24",
        "quote": "Around two-thirds of respondents reported confidence in their local police (67%) in the year ending March 2025, a slight increase from the previous year but lower than 76% a decade ago"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Concerns exist about politicisation of policing under a board model involving councillors with party affiliations.",
        "evidence_id": "E35",
        "quote": "concerns about politicising policing due to their political affiliations"
      },
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The abolition of PCCs requires legislation that has not yet been tabled, making implementation uncertain.",
        "evidence_id": "E36",
        "quote": "the decision to abolish PCCs requires legislation that has not yet been tabled"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "O9 covers democratic rights, accountability, and due process. This policy is centrally about the democratic governance structure for policing \u2014 who is accountable to whom for how the police are run.\n\nOn the side of worsening democratic rights: PCCs are directly elected, giving citizens a specific vote on policing accountability. Replacing them with boards of councillors nominated rather than directly elected for this role weakens that direct link. Critics \u2014 including the APCC and a sitting PCC \u2014 warn of an accountability vacuum and a loss of democratic mandate. Analysts predict these boards will be less visible and less effective at scrutiny. There is also a politicisation risk from councillors' party affiliations.\n\nOn the side of improving democratic rights: the current PCC model has demonstrably shallow democratic engagement \u2014 23% turnout and widespread public unawareness. Integrating policing oversight into existing elected local government structures, where councillors already have a broader democratic mandate across services, could embed accountability more durably in the local democratic fabric. The fall in public confidence in policing over the past decade (76% to 67%) suggests the existing model has not been effective at maintaining trust.\n\nThe verdict is mixed because both sides rest on cited evidence: the loss of a direct elected mandate is real (E2, E30, E31), and the weakness of the existing model is also real (E22, E24). The magnitude is minor because: (a) this is a governance restructuring, not a substantive change to legal rights or anti-discrimination protections; (b) the actual democratic effect depends heavily on how the new boards operate in practice; and (c) legislation has not yet been tabled (E36), so delivery is uncertain. No single direction dominates on the evidence provided.",
